---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import { nim } from "../../lib/site-indexes.mjs";
import { editions } from "../../lib/catalog.mjs";
const hasRulebook = editions.some(
  (e) => JSON.stringify(e) === JSON.stringify(nim.rulebook),
);
const report = nim.verification;
---

<Base title="Nim definition review | RuleBooks" active="model"
  ><section class="prose-page shell">
    <a href="/tools/reference/">← Reference tooling</a><p class="eyebrow">
      DRAFT GAME DEFINITION · ACCEPTANCE PENDING
    </p><h1>Nim, end to end.</h1><p class="reader-deck">
      Two heaps containing one and two counters. Player A starts; the player
      taking the final counter wins.
    </p><div class="scope-warning">
      <strong
        >Engineering verification passed; editorial acceptance is pending.</strong
      ><p>
        This is a candidate implementation of the existing Nim rulebook under
        experimental semantics. It does not yet establish an accepted
        Formalization or BGSM release.
      </p>
    </div><h2>What was checked</h2><p>{report.scope}</p><ul>
      <li>{report.bounds.reachableStates} reachable states</li><li>
        {report.bounds.legalTransitions} legal transitions
      </li><li>
        {report.bounds.rejectedIntents} rejected actor/parameter combinations, without
        state mutation
      </li><li>{report.bounds.terminalStates} terminal states</li>
    </ul><p>
      An independent Python oracle enumerates the expected moves and outcomes.
      The declarative definition exposes setup, turn ownership, heap selection,
      removal, state changes and ending conditions through reusable operations.
      There is no host primitive named Nim.
    </p><h2>Rule-to-source mapping</h2><ul>
      {
        nim.source.sourceMap.map((link) => (
          <li>
            <a
              href={
                hasRulebook
                  ? `/editions/nim-one-two/1/#rule-${link.rule}`
                  : nim.files.find((file) =>
                      file.url.endsWith("/rulebook.json"),
                    ).url
              }
            >
              {link.rule}
            </a>{" "}
            → <code>{link.node}</code>
          </li>
        ))
      }
    </ul><details>
      <summary>Inspect the complete authored definition</summary
      ><pre><code set:text={JSON.stringify(nim.source,null,2)} /></pre>
    </details><h2>Exact review inputs</h2><p>
      Rulebook: {report.rulebook.id}, revision {report.rulebook.revision}. The
      report pins its digest. Only this 1/2-heap scope has been exhaustively
      checked; the 3/4/5 profile and other variants are outside these results.
    </p><ul class="download-list">
      {
        nim.files.map((file) => (
          <li>
            <>
              <a href={file.url} download>
                {file.url.split("/").pop()}
              </a>
              <small>{file.bytes} bytes · SHA-256</small>
              <code>{file.sha256}</code>
            </>
          </li>
        ))
      }
    </ul><p>
      To publish this as accepted game data, the readable rules and semantic
      meaning still need accountable acceptance, followed by the existing
      Formalization, BGSMRelease and VerificationRecord publication contracts.
    </p>
  </section></Base
>
